          "kind": "prose",
          "id": "c2_explanation",
          "text": "A1 gives $ab\\ge0$, $4ab\\ge0$, and $a+b\\ge0$; with R1 this gives $0\\le4ab\\le(a+b)^2$. K2 applies with $u:=4ab$ and $v:=(a+b)^2$. At the auxiliary box, A1 again supplies the nonnegative conditions. The principal square root is the unique nonnegative number with the specified square. Since $2\\sqrt{ab}\\ge0$ and $(2\\sqrt{ab})^2=4ab$, its uniqueness gives $\\sqrt{4ab}=2\\sqrt{ab}$. Since $a+b\\ge0$, the same definition gives $\\sqrt{(a+b)^2}=a+b$. Substitute both identities into the main inequality. The final adjacent line divides by the positive number $2$, preserving the inequality direction."
        }
